Honeypot Architecture for Defensive Research
What honeypots actually are:
Honeypots are intentionally vulnerable systems deployed to attract attacker attention, allowing security teams to observe techniques, tools, and patterns without risking real infrastructure. They're a well-established defensive security practice.
Key Components of a Research Honeypot
1. Isolation & Safety
- Network isolation — honeypots sit on dedicated VLANs or isolated network segments
- No outbound access — they don't connect to real systems or fetch external content
- Resource limits — constrained CPU, memory, and disk to prevent them becoming attack platforms
- No user accounts — no legitimate services running, minimizing attack surface
2. What They Log
Typical honeypots capture:
- Connection metadata: Source IP, timestamps, protocols, ports
- Authentication attempts: Usernames tried, password attempts (common with SSH/Web honeypots)
- Command attempts: If interactive, what commands attackers run
- Payloads/tools: Binary downloads, exploit attempts, script executions
- User agents: What tools/software attackers claim to be
- File requests: What URLs or files they attempt to access
3. Common Honeypot Types
Type	Purpose	What It Mimics
SSH Honeypots (e.g., Cowrie)	Credential stuffing, botnet recruitment	Linux SSH servers
Web Honeypots	API scraping, vulnerability scanning	WordPress, phpMyAdmin, admin panels
Database Honeypots	SQL injection, data exfiltration attempts	MySQL, PostgreSQL endpoints
HTTP Proxy Honeypots	Proxy abuse, credential leaks	Open proxies, misconfigured servers
Cloud API Honeypots	Cloud credential theft, resource abuse	S3 buckets, Kubernetes APIs
4. Threat Intelligence Integration
Collected data flows into:
- Open source platforms (OTX, VirusTotal, AbuseIPDB)
- Commercial threat feeds (recorded Future, ThreatConnect)
- Industry ISACs (Information Sharing and Analysis Centers)
- Community projects (honeynet.org, project honey pot)
What gets shared (anonymized):
- Source IPs and ASN information
- Tool signatures and exploit patterns
- Timing and frequency of attacks
- Payload hashes (for signature creation)
- User agent strings and tool versions
5. Distinguishing Good vs. Bad Bots
Honeypots help classify traffic by:
- Legitimate crawlers — respect robots.txt, identify themselves, visit /robots.txt first
- Search engine bots — Googlebot, Bingbot, have valid reverse DNS
- Scrapers — ignore robots.txt, request at high frequency, don't identify
- Credential stuffers — try common usernames/passwords sequences
- Port scanners — scan systematic port ranges
- DDoS participants — coordinated timing, spoofed IPs
Indicators of malicious intent:
- User agents matching known exploit tools (sqlmap, nikto, nmap)
- User agents claiming to be browsers but making unusual requests
- Request patterns that don't match human behavior (no think time, exact intervals)
- IP reputation (known bad ASNs, data center ranges, Tor exits)
- User agent inconsistencies (claims Chrome but has Linux font rendering)
Getting Started Legally
If you want to explore this legitimately:
1. Use established honeypot platforms — Cowrie (SSH), Dionaea (malware), Glastopf (web application), or Honeyd
2. Deploy in isolated environments — cloud accounts with dedicated VPCs, not on production infrastructure
3. Review local laws — honeypot deployment regulations vary by jurisdiction
4. Document everything — maintain clear records of what you're collecting and why
5. Share appropriately — contribute to community projects while respecting privacy
